ealand Journal of Public Health, has found 88 per cent of Australian mothers are giving babies solid food before the age of six months.\nThe study also found 13 per cent of mothers were feeding babies solids at three months.\nLee King from the Australian Breastfeeding Association says the World Health Organisation recommends that babies have nothing but breast milk for six months.\n\"More and more research is being done and they've actually started to find that by increasing the breastfeeding and making sure that they only have exclusive breastfeeding, it can prevent obesity in later life,\" she said.\n\"But also there's a lot of other chronic illnesses and even common childhood illnesses and infections that babies actually have an increased risk of getting because they're getting solid food or being weaned too early.\"\nShe says inaccurate labelling on baby food encourages mothers to adopt solids at four months.\nMs King also says too many mothers are receiving conflicting advice about what to do if their babies start needing more food.\n\"When a baby is showing signs of wanting more or doing something different from normal, instead of actually looking for the reason or increasing her milk supply or asking for help, a lot of people just reach for the solid food,\" she said.\n\"Because the baby's not ready for it, that can set their system up for allergies and problems later.\"","tokens":["Solid"," food"," too"," early"," can"," lead"," to"," illness",","," study"," finds","\n","New"," research"," into"," breastfeeding"," mothers"," has"," found"," that"," 88"," per"," cent"," of"," babies"," are"," at"," risk"," of"," future"," health"," complications"," by"," the"," age"," of"," six"," months",".","\n","The"," Melbourne","-","based"," study",","," published"," in"," the"," Australian"," and"," New"," Zealand"," Journal"," of"," Public"," Health",","," has"," found"," 88"," per"," cent"," of"," Australian"," mothers"," are"," giving"," babies"," solid"," food"," be